{"name":"Speakeasy","hex":"#826a6c","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"speakeasy","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An illegal saloon or tavern, especially one operated during the American Prohibition period in the 1920s.","In modern times, a legal bar in the style of a speakeasy, i.e. one that is hidden away and has a quiet atmosphere."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/speakeasy"}],"entries":[{"title":"Speakeasy","blocks":[{"text":"A speakeasy, also called a beer flat, blind pig, or blind tiger, was an illicit establishment that sold alcoholic beverages. The term may also refer to a retro style bar that replicates aspects of historical speakeasies."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/21Club.JPG/330px-21Club.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Speakeasy"},{"title":"Speakeasy (computational environment)","blocks":[{"text":"Speakeasy was a numerical computing interactive environment also featuring an interpreted programming language. It was initially developed for internal use at the Physics Division of Argonne National Laboratory by the theoretical physicist Stanley Cohen. He eventually founded Speakeasy Computing Corporation to make the program available commercially."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d8/Speakeasy_Red_Logo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Speakeasy_(computational_environment)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Pittsburgh_Dispatch_Speakeasy_Origin.jpg/250px-Pittsburgh_Dispatch_Speakeasy_Origin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ac/Pittsburgh_Dispatch_Speakeasy_Origin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2